WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:01.230
Hi.

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:03.170
My name is Jenna Hong,
and I am a senior

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:06.290
at MIT studying brain and
cognitive sciences and computer

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:07.770
science and engineering.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:10.520
And my project is about
enhancing human memory

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.980
using augmented reality.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:15.650
I'm really interested
in reverse engineering

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:18.410
the science of intelligence
to better understand

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:21.290
abstract concepts such
as causal and moral

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:24.860
reasoning and intuitive
psychology and common sense

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:26.330
understanding,
which are all things

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:30.590
that we use to better digest
and process the world around us.

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:32.600
Here, at the Genesis
Group and CSAIL,

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:34.610
we believe that
human intelligence

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:37.550
is unique in our
natural capabilities

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:40.460
to tell and understand stories.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.620
I think it's really
interesting to study

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:45.950
how memory and learning
play a key role in this very

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:47.730
human storytelling.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:50.960
And so I based my research off
of various neuroscience studies

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.830
at MIT that elucidate how
memory works in patients

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:56.670
of Alzheimer's disease.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:00.440
So forms of dementia take the
ability to access and retrieve

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.620
memories away, but we can
still encode and store them

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:04.980
in the brain just fine.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.580
And so I study the
moment where computers

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:10.880
can help model and augment
our very natural learning

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:11.840
processes.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.470
I do my research in the Media
Lab in the Fluid Interfaces

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:18.110
group, where we're
working on building

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:20.750
a multi-modal augmented
reality system

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:24.290
that digitalizes something
called the memory palace, which

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:27.590
actually dates back to
ancient Greek philosophy.

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:30.440
The memory palace
leverages imagination.

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:32.630
By visualizing a
space or a location

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:36.650
that you know very well and
using mental images or visuals

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:40.010
and associating them on
particular points on the path,

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:43.070
you can better consolidate
these memories in your brain

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:46.180
just by re-walking the path
mentally or physically.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:49.310
As it turns out, spatial
navigation and episodic memory

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:52.880
both rely on the same area of
your brain, the hippocampus,

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:56.660
and so I use audio-visual
sensory cues to trigger

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:59.870
neural firing in the hippocampus
to better consolidate

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:01.880
these memories through space.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:03.900
And the results have
been really promising.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:07.130
Over the past year,
I've been carrying out

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:10.220
a series of human
behavioral experiments,

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:13.490
and it shows that the paradigm
which I call memory lane

00:02:13.490 --> 00:02:15.440
shows significant
improvement in retention

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.440
in both short-term memory
and long-term memory.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.540
By showing that sensory
cues can actually

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:23.690
help us encode memories
in our brain better,

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:25.370
we show that artificial
intelligence can

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:28.560
help us both understand
human cognition

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.710
but also unlock
further innovations

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:33.810
in assistive mixed reality.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:35.210
And so I hope that
this interface

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:40.670
can serve as a good first
step in empowering people

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:45.430
to overcome learning barriers
and memory impairments.

